.TH V3D 3
.SH NAME
v3d \- VideoCore IV QPUs, as far as the firmware exposes them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind -a #Q /dev
.PP
.B /dev/v3dctl
.br
.B /dev/v3dcode
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B v3d
device gives Limbo the Raspberry Pi firmware's two QPU property tags,
.B SET_ENABLE_QPU
and
.BR EXECUTE_QPU ,
and reports what the firmware answered.
.PP
.B "It is an instrument, not a driver."
A driver needs the QPU instruction encoding, and that encoding is not
documented on this machine. It has to be established from the
hardware, and this exists to make each attempt a write from Limbo
rather than a kernel rebuild and a card swap.
.PP
.B /dev/v3dcode
takes raw bytes, appended at a running offset so a program can be
copied in pieces.
.B /dev/v3dctl
takes:
.TP
.B reset
Discard the loaded code.
.TP
.B clock
Read the V3D clock
.RB ( GET_CLOCK_RATE ,
clock id 5).
.TP
.BR enable ", " disable
.B SET_ENABLE_QPU
with 1 or 0.
.TP
.BI exec " ctlidx w0 w1 w2 w3" " [" waitms ]
.B EXECUTE_QPU
with those four words as the request's value buffer, and the control
list's bus address substituted into word
.IR ctlidx .
.I Waitms
bounds the wait for a reply; the default is a second, which is what an
older spin count happened to cost.
.TP
.BI tag " TAG valbytes" " [" w0 ... ]
Any property tag, with that value-buffer size and up to four words.
Nothing is interpreted \- what a given tag expects is what is not
known.
.TP
.BI gpualloc " [" size ]
.B ALLOCATE_MEMORY
then
.BR LOCK_MEMORY ,
and
.B exec
then places the control list, uniforms and code inside the locked block
and sends the firmware's own bus address. A second
.B gpualloc
releases the first.
.TP
.B gpufree
Unlock and release it.
.PP
Reading
.B /dev/v3dctl
reports the clock, whether an enable was tried and succeeded, how much
code is loaded, the four words actually sent, and then what the
firmware answered.
.SH "WHAT THE STATUS MEANS"
.TP
.B code
The mailbox response:
.B 0x80000000
ok,
.B 0x80000001
error parsing the request.
.TP
.B tag
.B "The word that matters."
Bit 31 set means the tag was processed; the low bits are how many bytes
it wrote.
.B "A tag of 0 means EXECUTE_QPU was never touched,"
and
.B "wrote 0 bytes"
means it was processed and answered nothing.
.TP
.B value
.BR buf[5..8] " after the call,"
so an echo of the request can never be mistaken for an answer again.
.TP
.I us
Elapsed microseconds, from the board's own 1MHz counter.
.TP
.B wait
.BR answered ,
.B "OUT OF TIME (no reply)"
or
.BR "COUNTER NOT ADVANCING" .
Three different facts that a single \-1 used to flatten into one: a
mailbox bounded by a spin
.I count
cannot tell "has not answered yet" from "will never answer", and
reading one as the other is how a 1.05 second give-up was published as
the firmware working for a second.
.TP
.B "wrote N bytes"
.B "The discriminator."
An answered tag writes bytes and sets a length;
.B GET_CLOCK_RATE
writes 8. A processed bit with zero bytes is what both an absent tag
and
.B EXECUTE_QPU
produce under
.IR qemu (1),
so the bit alone separates nothing.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
bind -a '#Q' /dev
echo enable >/dev/v3dctl
cp prog.qpu /dev/v3dcode
echo exec 1 1 0 1 1000 >/dev/v3dctl
cat /dev/v3dctl
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /os/inferno/raspi3b/devv3d.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR qputry (1),
.IR draw3dkerntest (1),
.IR cons (3)
.SH BUGS
.B "Nothing here drives the GPU."
.IR Devdraw 's
.B gpudraw*
hooks are nil on this board, so the draw3d letters rasterise in
software. This device is how the question of whether they could do
otherwise is being investigated.
.PP
.B "An earlier version reported a"
.B resp
.B "word that was the caller's own input."
It read
.B buf[5]
unconditionally while writing the control address to
.BR buf[5+ctlidx] ,
so every figure published through it \- on silicon and under emulation
alike \- had to be withdrawn, along with a documented reading rule that
could never fire. The
.B value
line exists so that cannot recur.
.PP
.B "Under QEMU every answer here is fiction."
The emulator accepts tags it does not implement \- including
.BR 0x000f0f0f ,
which does not exist \- and fabricates values for ones it does: it
reports the V3D clock as 700MHz where silicon says 250. See
.I v3dprobe
in
.BR mailbox.c ,
which carries the nonsense-tag control.
.PP
.B SET_ENABLE_QPU
enables a power domain and nothing here disables it on close, so a
.B bind
and one
.B enable
leaves the block powered until reboot.
.PP
The
.B EXECUTE_QPU
argument layout is unknown. A believed
.RB ( num_qpus ,
.IR control ,
.IR noflush ,
.IR timeout_ms )
had its timeout falsified by a 500\(mu sweep that moved the elapsed
time by 0.004%, and
.I ctlidx
does not move it either. That is why
.B exec
takes all four words rather than named arguments.
.PP
One job only: the control list is built as a single
.RI ( uniforms ,
.IR code )
pair whatever
.I w0
says, so asking for several QPUs does not give each a different
program.
